takeaway co2: Release 100 GtC in 2050 and 50% of it is still in the air a century later; 41% after 250 years. Unlike other greenhouse gases (methane, nitrous oxide), which are chemically or photochemically destroyed, emitted carbon is not destroyed; it just moves between sinks. The result of this is that a share of carbon emissions is effectively permanent on a human timescale. 
fair note: <div class="caveat">FaIR is shown for the same pulse on the same scenario. Before 2025 this model follows the observed carbon budget and FaIR its own history, so the two release the pulse into slightly different worlds.</div> 
takeaway gmst: Release 100 GtC in 2050 and the world is 0.14 °C warmer a century later, and 0.14 °C warmer after 250 years. Over that stretch the warming keeps 104% of its century-later value and the airborne carbon keeps 81%, so the warming is the longer-lived of the two. 
permanence html:  
   What this says about carbon and temperature 
   
     This 100 GtC pulse warms the world by 0.135 °C a century later — 1.35 °C per 1000 GtC — and by 98% of its own peak warming still at 2300, 250 years after release. 
     
      Two things are worth separating here. The first is that the warming
      barely decays: a pulse of carbon emissions keeps warming the world for as
      long as this model runs, so on any human timescale the temperature
      resulting from a tonne of CO₂ is permanent.
     
     The effects of a pulse of carbon emissions on temperature are insensitive to the size of the pulse in this model. Across pulses from 10 to 500 GtC, the figure above moves by only 2.3%, and the impact on temperature is about 1.4 °C per 1000 GtC from any time period between 20 and 200 years after release. 
   
  
